WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:18.094
a few days, the new managers forgot about the ghost—until they received an envelope addressed in red ink.

2
00:00:19.555 --> 00:00:24.038
Richard opened the letter and read it aloud to his partner, Moncharmin.

3
00:00:26.799 --> 00:00:33.925
Dear Sirs: I am sorry to bother you when you must be busy reviewing the singers' and dancers'

4
00:00:34.025 --> 00:00:38.166
contracts, deciding whom to keep and whom to fire.

5
00:00:38.186 --> 00:00:45.228
I assume you realize that Carlotta's voice is nothing compared to Christine's.

6
00:00:46.529 --> 00:00:52.050
So I fail to understand why Christine has not been allowed to play the part of Marguerite in

7
00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:54.371
Faust since her triumph at the gala.

8
00:00:55.531 --> 00:01:02.261
However, I would still like to hear her tonight, when she'll perform her usual role of the boy

9
00:01:02.421 --> 00:01:09.024
Siébel. Therefore I ask you not to sell my box tonight or any other night.

10
00:01:10.965 --> 00:01:17.728
Several times when I've arrived at the opera, I have been disagreeably surprised to find that my box

11
00:01:17.768 --> 00:01:18.828
has been sold.

12
00:01:20.289 --> 00:01:26.591
I did not protest because I dislike scandal and also because I thought your predecessors had failed to

13
00:01:26.691 --> 00:01:28.372
mention my request.

14
00:01:29.852 --> 00:01:35.155
I wrote to them and have now received a reply, which proves that you are treating me with

15
00:01:35.235 --> 00:01:36.736
outrageous contempt.

16
00:01:38.117 --> 00:01:40.898
Please see the enclosed newspaper clipping.

17
00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:47.662
If you wish to live in peace, you must never take away my private box!

18
00:01:48.982 --> 00:01:59.328
Sincerely yours, The Opera Ghost The letter was accompanied by a personal ad cut from the newspaper: OG:

19
00:02:00.276 --> 00:02:03.478
There is no excuse for R M's behavior.

20
00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:08.022
We showed them your additions in the book.

21
00:02:08.282 --> 00:02:12.225
—D P Richard shook his head in disbelief.

22
00:02:12.245 --> 00:02:16.768
"Debienne and Poligny are still carrying on with their joke.

23
00:02:17.608 --> 00:02:20.891
They even went to the expense of putting an ad in the newspaper!"

24
00:02:20.911 --> 00:02:28.276
"They're harmless enough," Moncharmin said, "but what do they really want?

25
00:02:29.372 --> 00:02:31.654
A box for tonight's performance?"

26
00:02:32.975 --> 00:02:39.020
"Perhaps we should send them tickets for box five, if it's not already sold," Richard said.

27
00:02:40.221 --> 00:02:41.322
Moncharmin nodded.

28
00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:47.126
The two managers spent the rest of the day and evening negotiating contracts.

29
00:02:48.247 --> 00:02:54.252
Exhausted, they left the opera house without checking if their predecessors were enjoying the performance.

30
00:02:55.533 --> 00:03:03.494
The next morning they received a note from the opera ghost: Dear Sirs: Thank you.

31
00:03:04.135 --> 00:03:05.275
Charming evening.

32
00:03:06.536 --> 00:03:12.360
Will soon write about arrangements for your first payment of 20,000 francs.

33
00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:19.345
Kind regards, OG "This is outrageous!"

34
00:03:19.745 --> 00:03:20.746
Moncharmin said.

35
00:03:20.766 --> 00:03:25.369
"And look, here's a ridiculous reply from Debienne and Poligny.

36
00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.332
Apparently they didn't even come to the opera last night!"

37
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.843
Gentlemen: We are much obliged to you for thinking of us, but we cannot use tickets to box

38
00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:44.107
five. As we have explained, that box is exclusively reserved for the person whom we have discussed with

39
00:03:44.147 --> 00:03:53.773
you. Most sincerely, Debienne and Poligny Richard ripped up both notes and tossed them into a wastebasket.

40
00:03:53.793 --> 00:03:58.135
"These fellows are beginning to annoy me!

41
00:03:58.956 --> 00:04:02.742
As of tonight, we're selling box five's seats to anyone who wants them!"

42
00:04:03.522 --> 00:04:08.105
The following morning the managers found a security guard's report in their office.

43
00:04:08.125 --> 00:04:16.030
"There was some trouble with box five last night," Moncharmin said, summarizing the report for Richard.

44
00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:20.713
"The ticket holders didn't arrive until the beginning of the second act.

45
00:04:22.153 --> 00:04:26.036
The moment they entered the box, a voice said that the seats were taken.

46
00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:31.992
They saw no one, so they came out and called the usher, who checked the box and found

47
00:04:32.052 --> 00:04:32.713
it empty.

48
00:04:34.254 --> 00:04:37.977
When the patrons reentered the box, they heard the voice again.

49
00:04:39.158 --> 00:04:42.941
They consulted the usher, who said it must be the opera ghost.

50
00:04:43.922 --> 00:04:49.647
At which point, the ticket holders became loud and abusive, and the usher called the security guard, who

51
00:04:49.687 --> 00:04:50.668
removed them."

52
00:04:50.688 --> 00:04:54.351
"We need to speak to that usher right now!"

53
00:04:54.751 --> 00:04:55.592
Richard said.

54
00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:02.763
Moments later the usher, a woman in a black dress and faded shawl, entered the office.

55
00:05:02.783 --> 00:05:05.724
"What's your name?"

56
00:05:06.164 --> 00:05:07.085
Richard asked.

57
00:05:07.105 --> 00:05:09.006
"Madame Giry.

58
00:05:09.646 --> 00:05:15.149
I'm the mother of Meg, one of your best dancers," she said with obvious pride.

59
00:05:15.169 --> 00:05:18.610
"Never heard of her," Richard said.

60
00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:22.012
"Tell us what happened last night with box five."

61
00:05:23.444 --> 00:05:27.505
"I was planning to talk to you about box five, so you wouldn't have the same trouble as

62
00:05:27.565 --> 00:05:29.846
Monsieur Debienne and Monsieur Poligny.

63
00:05:31.006 --> 00:05:37.248
They wouldn't listen to me at first, and—" "Never mind that," Richard interrupted.

64
00:05:37.268 --> 00:05:41.809
"I'm asking what happened last night."

65
00:05:43.469 --> 00:05:49.291
Madame Giry turned purple with indignation because no one had ever spoken to her like that.

66
00:05:50.871 --> 00:05:52.612
She replied in a haughty voice.

67
00:05:53.872 --> 00:05:55.713
"I'll tell you what happened.

68
00:05:55.733 --> 00:05:58.895
The ghost was annoyed again!"

69
00:05:58.915 --> 00:06:00.756
"The ghost!

70
00:06:01.256 --> 00:06:09.681
Don't talk to me about that—" "Calm yourself, Richard," Moncharmin said, "and let me take over here."

71
00:06:11.422 --> 00:06:18.706
He turned to the usher and politely said, "Now, madame, tell us all about your ghost in box

72
00:06:18.826 --> 00:06:21.588
five." Madame Giry nodded.

73
00:06:22.544 --> 00:06:26.348
"No one ever sees him, but everybody can hear him.

74
00:06:27.449 --> 00:06:31.993
I've often heard him, and you can believe me because I always tell the truth."

75
00:06:33.374 --> 00:06:39.159
Behind her back, Richard gave his partner a skeptical look, but Moncharmin persisted.

76
00:06:39.179 --> 00:06:43.783
"Have you ever spoken to the ghost, my good lady?"

77
00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:46.125
"Oh, yes!

78
00:06:46.886 --> 00:06:49.208
Just like I'm speaking to you now, sir."

79
00:06:51.404 --> 00:06:55.008
"And when the ghost speaks to you, what does he say?"

80
00:06:55.528 --> 00:06:56.729
Moncharmin asked.

81
00:06:56.749 --> 00:07:01.413
"He usually asks me to bring him a program."

82
00:07:02.634 --> 00:07:03.775
Richard snorted.

83
00:07:03.796 --> 00:07:06.058
"A program?"

84
00:07:06.078 --> 00:07:11.262
"Well, I can't very well hand it to him when he arrives, can I?"

85
00:07:12.403 --> 00:07:14.385
Madame Giry glared at Richard.

86
00:07:14.405 --> 00:07:17.248
"I never see him arrive."

87
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.081
"How do you know the ghost is a man?"

88
00:07:21.661 --> 00:07:22.741
Moncharmin asked.

89
00:07:22.761 --> 00:07:28.143
"He has a man's voice, such a lovely voice."

90
00:07:29.283 --> 00:07:32.244
Madame Giry clasped her hands together in delight.

91
00:07:32.264 --> 00:07:37.525
"When he arrives, it's usually in the middle of the first act.

92
00:07:38.605 --> 00:07:41.906
He gives three little taps on the door of box five.

93
00:07:42.746 --> 00:07:47.568
The first time I heard that tapping, I was puzzled because I knew there was no one in

94
00:07:47.608 --> 00:07:48.128
the box.

95
00:07:49.352 --> 00:07:52.954
I opened the door, and the voice requested a program."

96
00:07:52.974 --> 00:07:55.495
"And how did you react?"

97
00:07:57.116 --> 00:08:01.819
"Well, I wasn't afraid even though the voice said it was the opera ghost.

98
00:08:03.019 --> 00:08:08.522
It spoke to me so kindly and mentioned my dear, departed husband."

99
00:08:08.542 --> 00:08:12.044
"Did you see the ghost leave the box?"

100
00:08:12.625 --> 00:08:13.585
Moncharmin said.

101
00:08:13.605 --> 00:08:21.466
"No, but after the performance, the program was gone, and the ghost had left me a tip."

102
00:08:21.486 --> 00:08:23.407
"A tip?"

103
00:08:24.808 --> 00:08:30.392
Richard rolled his eyes because her story was becoming more unbelievable every minute.

104
00:08:31.553 --> 00:08:34.275
"Yes, a tip," Madame Giry insisted.

105
00:08:34.295 --> 00:08:42.440
"Usually he leaves just a few francs, but once he left a box of English candy, which I'm

106
00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:43.401
very fond of.

107
00:08:44.482 --> 00:08:49.197
However, now that people are annoying him again, he leaves me nothing."

108
00:08:49.217 --> 00:08:54.560
"Thank you, that will do," Moncharmin said, indicating the door.

109
00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:02.944
As soon as Madame Giry had left, the managers agreed to fire her and investigate box five themselves.
